course: language
lesson: 8
title: Tenses: past, present and future
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Tense is the form of a verb that shows when something happens: I go (present), I went (past); time words like "yesterday" and "tomorrow" must match the verb, so "Yesterday I go to the zoo" needs "went".
explain: English has no special future ending on the verb, unlike Ukrainian or French; it uses helpers instead: "I will go", "I'm going to go", or even the present with a time word: "The train leaves at five".
explain: "I've eaten breakfast" links the past to now (so I'm not hungry); "I ate breakfast at 7" just tells you about a finished time; after "have" you need the special form: gone, eaten, seen, so it is "I have gone", never "I have went".
explain: "I was swimming when the lifeguard blew the whistle" mixes a long action going on (was swimming) with a short one that cut in (blew).
explain: Storytellers often say "So I'm walking down the road, and this dog comes up to me..." to make a past story feel live; and in Irish English "I'm after eating my dinner" means I have just eaten, not that I'm about to.
fact: Tense is the form of a verb that shows time; the past tense is for things that already happened.
fact: The historic present tells a past story in the present tense to make it exciting; in written stories you usually choose one tense and keep it.
fact: English talks about the future with will, going to, or the present plus a time word, like "I'm going tomorrow".
fact: Gonna is the spoken form of "going to"; "I was going to" shows a past plan that didn't happen; write "going to" in formal work.
fact: Ngl is texting slang for "not gonna lie", meaning honestly.
fact: The past participle is the verb form used after have, like gone, eaten and seen; have plus a past participle is called the present perfect.
fact: Use went on its own (I went) and gone after have (I have gone); "I have went" is heard in some dialects but not in standard English.
fact: The present perfect (I've eaten) links the past to now; the simple past (I ate) tells of a finished time; Americans often say "Did you eat yet?" where British speakers say "Have you eaten yet?"
fact: In Irish English "I'm after eating" means "I have just eaten"; this pattern comes from the Irish language.
fact: The past continuous is was or were plus an -ing verb, for an action in progress in the past: "I was swimming".
fact: Blow is irregular: blow, blew, blown.
fact: The future continuous is "will be" plus an -ing verb: "I will be swimming at 3 o'clock tomorrow".
fact: Indian English, used by millions, has its own patterns, such as "I am knowing him for years", where British and American English say "I have known him for years".
exercise: Owen writes "Yesterday I go to the zoo and I see a lion." What should it be? | Yesterday I went to the zoo and I saw a lion | Yesterday I goed to the zoo and I seed a lion | Yesterday I will go to the zoo and see a lion | Yesterday I going to the zoo and seeing a lion
exercise: What does tense show? | When something happens | Who does the action | How loud something is | Where something happens
exercise: A storyteller says "So I'm walking down the road and this dog comes up to me." Why the present tense? | To make a past story feel live and exciting | Because the story is happening right now as he talks | Because he forgot the past tense | Because dogs are always in the present
exercise: Mila says the future uses "will", Jakub says "going to". Who is right? | Both | Only Mila | Only Jakub | Neither, English uses a future ending
exercise: Which sentence talks about the future using the present tense? | The train leaves at five tomorrow | The train left at five yesterday | The train has left already | The train was leaving at five
exercise: A Gen Alpha boy says "I was gonna do my homework but I didn't, ngl." What does he mean? | Honestly, he planned to but didn't do it | He will do it tomorrow, and that's the honest truth | He did it, and it was easy | He never planned to do it
exercise: Anna writes "I have went to France." What should it be? | I have gone to France | I have go to France | I has went to France | I have goed to France
exercise: Which is the past participle of eat? | eaten | ate | eated | eating
exercise: Liam says "I've eaten breakfast." What does that suggest? | It links to now, so he's probably not hungry | He is eating his breakfast right now as he speaks | He is eating breakfast right now | He ate breakfast years ago and never since
exercise: Which is more common in American English? | Did you eat yet? | Have you eaten yet? | Are you eaten yet? | Did you eaten yet?
exercise: Ciara's Irish uncle says "I'm after eating my dinner." What does he mean? | He has just eaten his dinner | He is about to eat his dinner | He is chasing his dinner | He will eat after everyone else
exercise: Grace writes "I was swimming when the lifeguard blowed the whistle." What needs fixing? | blowed should be blew | was swimming should be swimmed | when should be because | Nothing at all
exercise: Which is the past continuous? | I was swimming | I swam | I will swim | I have swum
exercise: Kai says "I will be be swimming at 3." What's the fix? | I will be swimming at 3 | I will be be swim at 3 | I will swimming at 3 | I be will swimming at 3
exercise: An Indian English speaker says "I am knowing him for years." How would standard British English say it? | I have known him for years | I knew him for years tomorrow | I am know him for years | I will know him for years
exercise: How many verbs in "I was reading when the phone rang, so I have missed the end" are in the past continuous? | One: was reading | Two: was reading and rang | Three: was reading, rang, have missed | None at all
exercise: Which time word cannot go with "I have eaten"? | yesterday at 7 | already | just | today
test: Your mate writes "Last week we play footy and we win 3-0." What tense should he use? | The past: we played and we won | The future: we will play and win | The present: we plays and wins | It's fine, "last week" already shows the time
test: Your nan goes "gonna isn't a word, ya know." What's the fair answer? | It's spoken "going to"; fine in speech, write "going to" in essays | She's right, nobody anywhere has ever really said gonna | Gonna is the only correct form now | It means "gone to", so it's past tense
test: What would happen if English had no tenses or time words? | You couldn't easily tell if something happened, is happening or will happen | Nothing, verbs never show time | Only questions would make sense | All stories would be in the future
test: An Irish mate texts "I'm after finishing me homework, grand." ("Grand" means fine, OK in Ireland.) Has he finished? | Yes, he's just finished | No, he'll do it after tea | No, he's chasing it | He hasn't started it
test: A boomer uncle says "Back in my day I have went to school on foot." How do you put it right? | I went to school on foot | I have goed to school on foot | I gone to school on foot | I go to school on foot back then
test: In a video game, the cut-scene says "The dragon was sleeping when the knight crept in." Which action was going on already? | The dragon sleeping | The knight creeping in | Both started at the same time | Neither, it's the future
test: Your little cousin says English has no future at all "cos there's no future ending." What's true? | English shows the future with helpers like will and going to | He's right, English has no way at all to talk about tomorrow | English has a future ending -ed | English uses only "shall" for the future
test: An American friend asks "Did you finish your homework yet?" Your British gran would ask it how? | Have you finished your homework yet? | Did you finished your homework yet? | Are you finish your homework yet? | Have you finish your homework yet?
test: Your Indian English cousin says "Today I am having a cold." Did you understand her? | Yes, she has a cold today | No, she is buying a cold drink | No, she will have a cold later | Yes, she gave someone a cold
test: Dad asks "What'll you be doing at 3 tomorrow?" Which reply uses the future continuous? | I'll be swimming | I swam | I've swum | I was swimming
test: A Scouse kid says "I've went the shops, la." ("La" means lad, mate in Liverpool.) In written standard English, what is it? | I have gone to the shops | I has went to the shops | I have went to the shops | I gone to the shops
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the grammatical category of tense? | The form of a verb indicating the time of an action or state | The form of a noun indicating number | The order in which adjectives are arranged | The punctuation concluding a sentence
exam: Identify the principal means whereby English expresses future time. | Auxiliary forms such as "will" and "going to", or the present with an adverb of time | A distinct inflectional ending appended to the verb | The exclusive employment of the word "tomorrow" | English possesses no means of expressing future time
exam: Ascertain the correct form of the sentence "I have went to France" in standard English. | I have gone to France | I have go to France | I has gone to France | I have goed to France
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Irish English construction "I'm after eating my dinner". | It signifies that the speaker has recently eaten | It signifies that the speaker intends to eat presently | It signifies that the speaker is in pursuit of the meal | It is devoid of meaning in all varieties of English
